1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,872
The AI says done.

2
00:00:01,872 --> 00:00:08,236
But I still open the app, click through each requirement, capture failures, and explain them back.

3
00:00:08,236 --> 00:00:11,231
After every edit, I repeat the work.

4
00:00:11,231 --> 00:00:14,600
Ming keeps acceptance checks and their evidence together.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,284
The requirement here is simple: submitted reports must survive a refresh.

6
00:00:20,284 --> 00:00:24,687
I review the conditions and their dependencies before confirming.

7
00:00:24,687 --> 00:00:29,090
This demonstration uses a clearly labeled, hand authored plan.

8
00:00:29,090 --> 00:00:31,291
Browser execution is real.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,467
This sample contains a deliberately seeded bug.

10
00:00:35,467 --> 00:00:39,800
I start acceptance, and Ming operates a real browser.

11
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:44,567
Submission works, but persistence fails: the report disappears after refresh.

12
00:00:44,567 --> 00:00:47,600
The other two checks still pass.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,685
The interface connects the requirement to the actual steps, expected result, and observed failure.

14
00:00:53,685 --> 00:00:57,475
I can inspect the screenshot and the failed assertion.

15
00:00:57,475 --> 00:01:02,781
Each run retains its own evidence, so a later result cannot replace it.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,424
I create a repair task from this failure.

17
00:01:07,424 --> 00:01:11,229
It is waiting for an agent, not already repaired.

18
00:01:11,229 --> 00:01:15,415
The English handoff contains the task, baseline and unchanged plan.

19
00:01:15,415 --> 00:01:19,600
A connected coding agent retrieves the evidence through Ming MCP.

20
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,805
Now I open a repair recorded earlier, using the same logical target before and after.

21
00:01:25,805 --> 00:01:32,335
Codex retrieved this evidence through the real MCP tools, claimed the task, and changed the
application source.

22
00:01:32,335 --> 00:01:35,600
This is a review of that saved repair.

23
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,024
The comparison checks that the plan and runner match, while the source fingerprint changes.

24
00:01:42,024 --> 00:01:44,836
The original persistence failure now passes.

25
00:01:44,836 --> 00:01:51,262
All three criteria pass, and the original failed run remains available beside the new result.

26
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,317
These are the original before and after browser images.

27
00:01:55,317 --> 00:01:58,966
I can open the repaired run and inspect each criterion.

28
00:01:58,966 --> 00:02:01,951
Ming verifies this repair against the registered scope.

29
00:02:01,951 --> 00:02:05,600
It does not claim that every possible behavior is correct.

30
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,272
A separate task manager uses different markup and its own acceptance plan.

31
00:02:11,272 --> 00:02:16,139
The same runner checks task creation, completion, and persistence after refresh.

32
00:02:16,139 --> 00:02:20,600
This second project demonstrates reuse beyond the daily report sample.

33
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,362
The model provider is configurable.

34
00:02:23,362 --> 00:02:28,478
No live model call or API charge is used in this demonstration.

35
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,778
Bob implemented the core runner, evidence history, model adapter and repair foundations.

36
00:02:34,778 --> 00:02:39,557
Codex completed the interface and integrity checks, and performed the recorded repair.

37
00:02:39,557 --> 00:02:43,600
The original Bob task summaries are included in the repository.
